01:05:35,000 --> 01:05:38,200
Why did she
say that?

1012
01:05:38,240 --> 01:05:40,760
Oh, don't pretend you
don't know about me.

1013
01:05:40,800 --> 01:05:42,200
Everybody knows.
Everybody!

1014
01:05:42,240 --> 01:05:45,280
Look, h-honestly,
I-- I don't know anything.

1015
01:05:45,320 --> 01:05:47,920
I remember hearing
something...

1016
01:05:47,960 --> 01:05:51,680
a few years ago
when we first moved here, but--

1017
01:05:51,720 --> 01:05:53,600
something about
a-- a shooting

1018
01:05:53,640 --> 01:05:55,720
at the mall.

1019
01:05:55,760 --> 01:05:59,240
That's-- that's it.
That's all.

1020
01:05:59,280 --> 01:06:01,480
I didn't even
know you then.

1021
01:06:05,840 --> 01:06:07,800
I'm sorry.

1022
01:06:10,000 --> 01:06:11,880
Sorry.

1023
01:06:12,880 --> 01:06:14,920
It's okay.

1024
01:06:20,640 --> 01:06:24,120
Dispatch said there
was a shooter loose at the mall.

1025
01:06:24,160 --> 01:06:26,280
It was 10 minutes
from the end of my shift.

1026
01:06:26,320 --> 01:06:28,800
10 minutes and there would have
been someone else.

1027
01:06:31,680 --> 01:06:34,400
I can still see that
boy's face staring up at me.

1028
01:06:35,360 --> 01:06:38,840
Yeah, but--
but it was an accident.

1029
01:06:38,880 --> 01:06:40,800
You were trying
to stop a guy,

1030
01:06:40,880 --> 01:06:44,120
and the-- the boy
g-got caught

1031
01:06:44,160 --> 01:06:45,640
in the crossfire,
right?

1032
01:06:45,680 --> 01:06:47,400
No, I panicked.

1033
01:06:47,440 --> 01:06:49,320
There was no shooter.

1034
01:06:49,360 --> 01:06:51,240
Just the boy.

1035
01:06:51,280 --> 01:06:54,280
Antoine Harris
was his name.

1036
01:06:54,320 --> 01:06:57,120
He was big for his age,
only 13 years old.

1037
01:06:57,160 --> 01:06:59,880
He was a good kid.
Thought it was a joke,

1038
01:06:59,920 --> 01:07:01,760
waving around an air gun
at his friend

1039
01:07:01,800 --> 01:07:03,400
in the Big 5.

1040
01:07:03,440 --> 01:07:06,320
They were acting out some scene
from some movie they liked.

1041
01:07:06,320 --> 01:07:08,280
Shop girl saw it
from across the way,

1042
01:07:08,320 --> 01:07:11,680
called 911.

1043
01:07:11,760 --> 01:07:14,560
Jesus, that's terrible.

1044
01:07:14,600 --> 01:07:17,080
That...

1045
01:07:17,120 --> 01:07:19,560
well, you didn't know.

1046
01:07:19,600 --> 01:07:21,560
It could have
been real.

1047
01:07:21,600 --> 01:07:23,560
But it wasn't.

1048
01:07:29,160 --> 01:07:31,720
His parents...

1049
01:07:31,760 --> 01:07:34,240
uh, his parents--

1050
01:07:36,960 --> 01:07:38,880
I had to...

1051
01:07:55,640 --> 01:07:57,920
I was diagnosed with post-traumatic
stress syndrome

1052
01:07:57,960 --> 01:08:00,960
by three different
psychiatrists.

1053
01:08:01,000 --> 01:08:03,680
That's why I retired.

1054
01:08:03,720 --> 01:08:06,400
I couldn't do
the job anymore.

1055
01:08:11,640 --> 01:08:14,280
- So why don't you do something else?
- Like what?

1056
01:08:14,320 --> 01:08:17,159
Drive a forklift
at Costco?

1057
01:08:19,000 --> 01:08:22,119
Maybe you could
go back to school.

1058
01:08:22,159 --> 01:08:24,119
I loved my job.

1059
01:08:24,159 --> 01:08:26,320
I don't wanna do
anything else.

1060
01:08:38,559 --> 01:08:41,920
You ever think about the term
"homeland security"?

1061
01:08:43,599 --> 01:08:45,520
I mean,
really think about it?

1062
01:08:51,440 --> 01:08:53,320
The day that
you found out

1063
01:08:53,360 --> 01:08:56,639
that your father had
been killed in Iraq,

1064
01:08:56,680 --> 01:08:58,599
do you remember
that day?

1065
01:08:58,639 --> 01:09:00,159
Can you talk
about that?

1066
01:09:00,200 --> 01:09:02,240
You feel comfortable
talking about that?

1067
01:09:06,480 --> 01:09:10,200
After the men came

1068
01:09:10,240 --> 01:09:12,720
to tell my mom,

1069
01:09:12,760 --> 01:09:15,040
I cried,

1070
01:09:15,079 --> 01:09:18,440
but she didn't.

1071
01:09:18,480 --> 01:09:21,000
She just went up
to her room

1072
01:09:21,040 --> 01:09:24,800
and grabbed the pillows
off the bed,

1073
01:09:24,840 --> 01:09:27,680
cut the tops off of them
with a pair scissors.

1074
01:09:30,000 --> 01:09:33,119
There were feathers
all over the place.

1075
01:09:34,440 --> 01:09:36,520
That must have really
frightened you.

1076
01:09:37,960 --> 01:09:41,119
No, she was trying
to find the crown.

1077
01:09:42,159 --> 01:09:43,720
The crown?

1078
01:09:43,720 --> 01:09:45,639
The crown you leave
in your pillow

1079
01:09:45,639 --> 01:09:48,040
when you've slept on it
for a long time.

1080
01:09:50,320 --> 01:09:52,599
My--

1081
01:09:52,639 --> 01:09:55,200
my father had
two crowns.

1082
01:09:55,240 --> 01:09:58,440
Stop there.

1083
01:09:58,480 --> 01:10:01,560
- You hungry?
- No.

1084
01:10:01,600 --> 01:10:03,320
Mind if I get
some lunch?

1085
01:10:03,360 --> 01:10:06,080
No, go ahead.

1086
01:10:26,680 --> 01:10:28,680
Brad?

1087
01:10:28,720 --> 01:10:32,040
Honey, you there?

1088
01:10:32,080 --> 01:10:35,560
Pick up.
I know you're there.

1089
01:10:35,600 --> 01:10:38,040
It's Aaron's nap time.

1090
01:10:41,280 --> 01:10:43,680
Okay, I guess you guys
are out somewhere.

1091
01:10:43,720 --> 01:10:46,080
Um...

1092
01:10:46,120 --> 01:10:48,640
I love you both.

1093
01:10:48,680 --> 01:10:50,680
Bye.

1094
01:11:36,040 --> 01:11:38,760
I certainly did.

1095
01:11:38,800 --> 01:11:41,000
Here we go.

1096
01:11:42,640 --> 01:11:44,520
I don't know, Jean.
I don't think I'm up for this.

1097
01:11:44,560 --> 01:11:46,280
Well, now don't
be silly.

1098
01:11:46,320 --> 01:11:48,360
- It'll be fun.
- Really?

1099
01:11:48,400 --> 01:11:50,560
You're not the only
little sister here tonight.

1100
01:11:50,600 --> 01:11:54,000
- Oh, that's good.
- Oh!

1101
01:11:54,040 --> 01:11:56,000
Hi, would you
like a glass?

1102
01:11:56,080 --> 01:11:59,560
Did anybody like this book?
Because I really just hated it.

1103
01:11:59,600 --> 01:12:01,360
It's so depressing.

1104
01:12:01,400 --> 01:12:03,400
She cheats on her husband
with two different guys,

1105
01:12:03,440 --> 01:12:06,000
wastes all his money,
then kills herself with rat poison?

1106
01:12:06,040 --> 01:12:08,040
Do I really need
to read this?

1107
01:12:08,080 --> 01:12:11,520
Well, there is a lot
of good descriptive writing.

1108
01:12:11,560 --> 01:12:13,480
It's supposed
to be depressing.

1109
01:12:13,480 --> 01:12:15,360
It's a tragedy.

1110
01:12:15,400 --> 01:12:18,680
Madame Bovary was undone
by a tragic flaw.

1111
01:12:18,720 --> 01:12:20,560
What's her flaw?

1112
01:12:20,600 --> 01:12:22,480
Blindness.
She didn't see

1113
01:12:22,520 --> 01:12:24,560
that the men were
just using her.

1114
01:12:24,600 --> 01:12:27,080
She just wants
a little romance in her life.

1115
01:12:27,120 --> 01:12:30,640
- You can't blame her for that.
- It's about women's choices.

1116
01:12:30,680 --> 01:12:33,280
Back then, a woman
didn't have a lot of choices.

1117
01:12:33,320 --> 01:12:35,400
You could be a nun
or a wife.

1118
01:12:35,440 --> 01:12:38,920
- That's all there was.
- Or a prostitute.

1119
01:12:38,960 --> 01:12:40,040
She had a choice.

1120
01:12:40,080 --> 01:12:42,640
She had a choice not
to cheat on her husband.

1121
01:12:42,680 --> 01:12:46,280
I found it refreshing
to read about a woman

1122
01:12:46,320 --> 01:12:48,120
reclaiming her sexuality.

1123
01:12:48,160 --> 01:12:50,320
Is that a nice way
of saying she's a slut?

1124
01:12:50,360 --> 01:12:52,880
Madame Bovary's
not a slut.

1125
01:12:52,920 --> 01:12:56,680
She's one of the great
characters in Western literature.

1126
01:12:56,720 --> 01:13:00,120
I was a little puzzled
by some of the sexual references.

1127
01:13:00,160 --> 01:13:01,800
Look,
like this one.

1128
01:13:01,840 --> 01:13:04,240
Um...

1129
01:13:04,280 --> 01:13:06,760
"He abandoned
every last shred

1130
01:13:06,800 --> 01:13:08,680
of restraint
and consideration.

1131
01:13:08,720 --> 01:13:12,080
He turned her
into something compliant,

1132
01:13:12,120 --> 01:13:14,560
something corrupt."

1133
01:13:18,400 --> 01:13:20,560
Does anyone know
what that means?

1134
01:13:20,600 --> 01:13:22,280
It means
she's a slut.

1135
01:13:22,320 --> 01:13:24,200
Is he tying her up

1136
01:13:24,240 --> 01:13:26,240
or something?

1137
01:13:26,320 --> 01:13:28,720
Anal sex.

1138
01:13:28,760 --> 01:13:30,920
Mm-hmm.
Mm-hmm.

1139
01:13:30,960 --> 01:13:34,480
Did everyone get that
but me?

1140
01:13:34,520 --> 01:13:37,320
Let's set that aside
for now.

1141
01:13:37,360 --> 01:13:39,240
I'm really eager
to hear what

1142
01:13:39,280 --> 01:13:42,000
our other little sister
has to say.

1143
01:13:42,040 --> 01:13:44,560
I'm not sure if you know this,
but Sarah has a Ph.D

1144
01:13:44,600 --> 01:13:47,400
in English lit.

1145
01:13:47,440 --> 01:13:49,480
Just a masters.

1146
01:13:49,480 --> 01:13:51,400
I never wrote
my dissertation.

1147
01:13:51,400 --> 01:13:54,440
Well, you still have
a lot more expertise

1148
01:13:54,480 --> 01:13:56,680
than the rest of us.

1149
01:14:01,320 --> 01:14:03,920
I think I understand
your feelings about this book.

1150
01:14:03,960 --> 01:14:07,400
I used to have some
problems with it myself.

1151
01:14:07,440 --> 01:14:09,320
When I read it
in grad school,

1152
01:14:09,360 --> 01:14:12,240
Madame Bovary just
seemed like a fool.

1153
01:14:12,280 --> 01:14:14,520
She marries
the wrong man,

1154
01:14:14,560 --> 01:14:18,040
makes one foolish mistake
after another.

1155
01:14:18,080 --> 01:14:21,640
But when I read it this time,
I just fell in love with her.

1156
01:14:21,680 --> 01:14:24,240
She's trapped.

1157
01:14:24,280 --> 01:14:27,080
She has a choice. She can either
accept a life of misery

1158
01:14:27,080 --> 01:14:30,520
or she can struggle
against it.

1159
01:14:30,560 --> 01:14:32,720
And she chooses
to struggle.

1160
01:14:32,760 --> 01:14:34,960
Some struggle--
hop into bed

1161
01:14:35,000 --> 01:14:36,880
with every guy
who says hello.

1162
01:14:36,920 --> 01:14:40,320
Well, she fails in the end, but there's
something beautiful

1163
01:14:40,360 --> 01:14:43,680
and even heroic
in her rebellion.

1164
01:14:43,760 --> 01:14:46,120
My professors would
kill me for even thinking this,

1165
01:14:46,120 --> 01:14:48,240
but...

1166
01:14:48,280 --> 01:14:50,680
in her own
strange way,

1167
01:14:50,720 --> 01:14:52,600
Emma Bovary
is a feminist.

1168
01:14:52,640 --> 01:14:54,480
Oh, that's nice.

1169
01:14:54,520 --> 01:14:57,240
So now cheating on your husband
makes you a feminist?

1170
01:14:57,280 --> 01:14:59,800
No no no.

1171
01:14:59,840 --> 01:15:03,280
It's not the cheating.
It's the hunger.

1172
01:15:03,320 --> 01:15:05,640
The hunger
for an alternative,

1173
01:15:05,640 --> 01:15:08,880
and the refusal to accept
a life of unhappiness.

1174
01:15:15,480 --> 01:15:19,000
Maybe I didn't
understand the book.

1175
01:15:21,120 --> 01:15:22,960
She just looks
so pathetic.

1176
01:15:24,640 --> 01:15:26,440
Is she pretty?

1177
01:15:27,920 --> 01:15:29,640
Who?

1178
01:15:32,400 --> 01:15:33,960
Your wife.

1179
01:15:34,000 --> 01:15:35,880
Degrading yourself
for nothing.

1180
01:15:35,920 --> 01:15:38,480
It's a simple question.

1181
01:15:38,520 --> 01:15:40,800
She's pretty, okay?
Do we have to talk about this now?

1182
01:15:42,920 --> 01:15:46,280
I mean, did she really think a man like
that was gonna run away with her?

1183
01:15:49,960 --> 01:15:52,640
Possibly.

1184
01:15:54,040 --> 01:15:55,920
How pretty is she?

1185
01:16:01,000 --> 01:16:02,920
A knockout.

1186
01:16:07,160 --> 01:16:09,200
Beauty's overrated,
Sarah.

1187
01:16:14,080 --> 01:16:17,080
Brad had meant this
to be comforting.

1188
01:16:17,120 --> 01:16:20,680
But at 3:00 in the morning, it had
precisely the opposite effect.

1189
01:16:22,600 --> 01:16:24,800
He had a beautiful wife,

1190
01:16:24,840 --> 01:16:28,400
a knockout, and she was sleeping
beside him right now.

1191
01:16:29,960 --> 01:16:32,000
Only someone who took
his own beauty for granted

1192
01:16:32,040 --> 01:16:34,600
would have been able
to say something so stupid

1193
01:16:34,640 --> 01:16:36,680
and with a straight face.

1194
01:16:59,680 --> 01:17:02,280
Weekends were difficult
for Sarah.

1195
01:17:02,320 --> 01:17:03,880
48-hour prison stretches

1196
01:17:03,920 --> 01:17:06,720
separating one happy blur
of weekdays

1197
01:17:06,760 --> 01:17:08,760
from the rest.

1198
01:17:11,080 --> 01:17:13,880
I'm going to the ocean.

1199
01:17:13,920 --> 01:17:16,520
- You are?
- I'm going to the ocean.

1200
01:17:16,560 --> 01:17:18,760
Is he doing
his ocean dance?

1201
01:17:23,280 --> 01:17:26,120
Sarah sometimes let herself
be carried away by fantasies

1202
01:17:26,160 --> 01:17:29,800
of a future very different from the life
she was living now,

1203
01:17:29,800 --> 01:17:32,040
in which she and Brad were
free to love each other

1204
01:17:32,080 --> 01:17:33,800
in broad daylight;

1205
01:17:33,840 --> 01:17:35,480
where they had no one
to answer to

1206
01:17:35,520 --> 01:17:37,800
but each other.

1207
01:17:37,840 --> 01:17:41,440
It could happen,
she thought. It had to,

1208
01:17:41,480 --> 01:17:43,840
because she wasn't sure she
could keep living like this

1209
01:17:43,880 --> 01:17:46,280
for very much longer.

1210
01:17:52,680 --> 01:17:55,120
Open your mouth again.

1211
01:17:55,160 --> 01:17:57,960
No, I don't want to.

1212
01:17:58,000 --> 01:18:00,800
May your mouth
be open.

1213
01:18:00,840 --> 01:18:02,720
May your mouth.

1214
01:18:02,800 --> 01:18:05,160
This is coming
into your mouth.

1215
01:18:07,080 --> 01:18:09,680
Open your mouth,
little fishy.

1216
01:18:09,720 --> 01:18:12,000
Hungry, hungry.

1217
01:18:13,440 --> 01:18:16,040
Little fishy
is not hungry?

1218
01:18:18,840 --> 01:18:20,800
You okay?

1219
01:18:22,280 --> 01:18:24,560
Yeah, fine.

1220
01:18:26,280 --> 01:18:28,640
- How about you?
- Great.

1221
01:18:32,280 --> 01:18:34,240
So how was
your weekend?

1222
01:18:37,160 --> 01:18:39,560
You really wanna know?
It sucked.

1223
01:18:41,600 --> 01:18:43,440
How was yours?

1224
01:18:43,480 --> 01:18:45,520
Terrible.

1225
01:18:46,560 --> 01:18:48,800
- Really?
- Yeah.

1226
01:18:50,760 --> 01:18:53,080
Yeah, we went to the beach
and all Kathy and I did

1227
01:18:53,120 --> 01:18:55,720
was argue
the whole time.

1228
01:18:55,760 --> 01:18:59,040
Oh, you did?

1229
01:19:01,400 --> 01:19:03,280
Yeah, it was our
annual argument

1230
01:19:03,320 --> 01:19:06,160
over taking
the bar exam,

1231
01:19:06,200 --> 01:19:08,160
like our whole life
depends on it.

1232
01:19:08,200 --> 01:19:10,560
Get it over with.
You'll feel better.

1233
01:19:10,600 --> 01:19:12,680
It's this whole thing.

1234
01:19:12,720 --> 01:19:15,080
Gotta take a train
on Wednesday.

1235
01:19:15,080 --> 01:19:17,880
It's a two-day ordeal.

1236
01:19:17,920 --> 01:19:20,200
I'm not even
gonna pass.

1237
01:19:20,200 --> 01:19:22,600
- You'll be fine.
- No.

1238
01:19:22,600 --> 01:19:26,400
No, I won't.

1239
01:19:26,440 --> 01:19:28,760
I haven't cracked
a book all summer.

1240
01:19:37,480 --> 01:19:40,200
I missed you.

1241
01:19:40,200 --> 01:19:42,840
I missed you, too.

1242
01:19:47,880 --> 01:19:50,280
Don't-- don't do it.

1243
01:19:51,360 --> 01:19:53,480
- What?
- The test.

1244
01:19:53,480 --> 01:19:56,120
- Oh.
- Blow it off.

1245
01:19:56,160 --> 01:19:58,560
We should go somewhere,

1246
01:19:58,600 --> 01:20:00,480
just for a night,
you know?

1247
01:20:00,520 --> 01:20:02,280
Richard's out
of town till Friday,

1248
01:20:02,280 --> 01:20:04,320
and I'm sure I can get
a sitter for Lucy.

1249
01:20:08,040 --> 01:20:10,360
No, I can't do that.

1250
01:20:10,400 --> 01:20:12,880
I gotta take the test.

1251
01:20:23,320 --> 01:20:25,800
That's good.

1252
01:20:25,840 --> 01:20:28,280
- Looks good?
- Yeah.

1253
01:20:28,320 --> 01:20:30,240
Well, I have a good
feeling about this whole thing.

1254
01:20:30,280 --> 01:20:33,120
In fact, I think I'm gonna go buy
a bottle of champagne

1255
01:20:33,160 --> 01:20:35,400
and put it in the fridge,
and we can open it to celebrate

1256
01:20:35,440 --> 01:20:37,880
when we get
the good news.

1257
01:20:37,920 --> 01:20:40,800
Don't get your hopes up.

1258
01:20:40,840 --> 01:20:42,760
We've been
through this before.

1259
01:20:42,760 --> 01:20:45,640
Huh-uh. This time's
gonna be different.

1260
01:20:45,680 --> 01:20:47,680
I can feel it.

1261
01:21:16,960 --> 01:21:20,200
Can you believe it?
This is our first date.

1262
01:21:20,240 --> 01:21:23,360
Like a date, you know,
without the kids, I mean.

1263
01:21:23,400 --> 01:21:26,480
How was Lucy?
Did she cry or anything?

1264
01:21:26,520 --> 01:21:28,600
No, are you kidding?

1265
01:21:28,640 --> 01:21:31,280
With Jean there, she
just about shoved me out the door.

1266
01:21:35,600 --> 01:21:39,360
I thought we could make something
really beautiful.

1267
01:21:39,400 --> 01:21:42,440
I have so
many things here.

1268
01:21:42,440 --> 01:21:45,000
We could make...

1269
01:21:45,040 --> 01:21:47,280
a picture frame.

1270
01:21:48,760 --> 01:21:52,600
Or a jewelry box.

1271
01:21:53,640 --> 01:21:57,480
Or a hat.

1272
01:21:59,400 --> 01:22:02,440
Something for my mommy.

1273
01:22:02,440 --> 01:22:04,360
Okay.

1274
01:22:07,080 --> 01:22:08,440
- Stop it, stop it.
- No, Ronnie.

1275
01:22:08,480 --> 01:22:11,000
Ron-- Ron-- Ronnie,
just hold on.

1276
01:22:11,040 --> 01:22:14,080
Now there.

1277
01:22:14,120 --> 01:22:17,440
You look handsome.
She won't be disappointed.

1278
01:22:17,480 --> 01:22:20,360
Wait'll she hears
about my criminal record.

1279
01:22:20,400 --> 01:22:22,600
I don't think you need
to get into that just yet.

1280
01:22:22,640 --> 01:22:24,800
Why don't you stick
to small talk?

1281
01:22:24,840 --> 01:22:27,680
What if someone
recognizes me?

1282
01:22:27,720 --> 01:22:31,040
That's highly unlikely.
I made the dinner reservation

1283
01:22:31,080 --> 01:22:33,440
at a restaurant over
in Haverhill.

1284
01:22:49,640 --> 01:22:51,560
Sheila.

1285
01:22:53,880 --> 01:22:56,120
- Sheila.
- Yeah.

1286
01:22:59,440 --> 01:23:01,800
Something wrong
with the food?

1287
01:23:03,320 --> 01:23:05,520
No, it's fine.

1288
01:23:05,560 --> 01:23:09,320
Back at the house you
mentioned you were on medication.

1289
01:23:11,600 --> 01:23:15,000
- What kind?
- All kinds.

1290
01:23:15,040 --> 01:23:16,960
Mostly psychotropic.

1291
01:23:19,920 --> 01:23:23,240
So you had some kind
of a breakdown?

1292
01:23:23,240 --> 01:23:26,880
Mm-hmm, my junior year
in college.

1293
01:23:26,920 --> 01:23:28,880
You were that young?

1294
01:23:30,800 --> 01:23:32,840
What happened?

1295
01:23:32,880 --> 01:23:35,480
I don't really know.

1296
01:23:35,520 --> 01:23:38,560
Well, nervous breakdowns
don't just come out of nowhere.

1297
01:23:39,760 --> 01:23:42,040
Something must have
caused it.

1298
01:23:42,080 --> 01:23:44,600
I guess.

1299
01:23:44,640 --> 01:23:47,480
But I was fine before
I left for college.

1300
01:23:49,000 --> 01:23:51,280
I don't know.
Maybe it was the stress

1301
01:23:51,320 --> 01:23:53,080
of being on my own.

1302
01:23:53,120 --> 01:23:55,760
Maybe it's a chemical
imbalance in my brain.

1303
01:23:55,800 --> 01:23:58,920
Every psychiatrist I go to
has a different opinion.

1304
01:23:58,960 --> 01:24:02,000
This one guy, Dr. Faris,

1305
01:24:02,040 --> 01:24:05,680
he said I must have been
sexually abused as a child.

1306
01:24:05,720 --> 01:24:07,200
When I told him
I wasn't,

1307
01:24:07,240 --> 01:24:09,720
he said I must be
repressing the memory.

1308
01:24:09,760 --> 01:24:13,400
Right, um, so what
happened after that?

1309
01:24:13,440 --> 01:24:15,240
Did you drop out
of school?

1310
01:24:15,240 --> 01:24:17,960
Not right away.

1311
01:24:18,000 --> 01:24:20,080
My mother wanted me
to go

1312
01:24:20,120 --> 01:24:22,080
to the campus
counseling center.

1313
01:24:22,120 --> 01:24:24,560
And they wanted
the problem fixed, like I could just

1314
01:24:24,600 --> 01:24:27,400
snap my fingers and everything
would be okay again.

1315
01:24:27,440 --> 01:24:29,800
Yeah, I know
all about that.

1316
01:24:29,800 --> 01:24:33,160
You do?

1317
01:24:33,200 --> 01:24:36,560
Yes, I do.

1318
01:24:39,560 --> 01:24:43,120
So they said
I could leave school

1319
01:24:43,160 --> 01:24:45,760
and get married and have lots of kids
like my sisters,

1320
01:24:45,800 --> 01:24:47,840
and I can't take care
of kids.

1321
01:24:47,880 --> 01:24:50,120
I can't even take care
of myself

1322
01:24:50,160 --> 01:24:53,200
most of the time.
Besides, who's gonna marry me?

1323
01:24:53,240 --> 01:24:56,520
You're not so bad.

1324
01:24:56,560 --> 01:24:58,520
What?

1325
01:24:59,680 --> 01:25:02,120
You're--
you're not so bad.

1326
01:25:07,720 --> 01:25:10,080
I haven't had
a real boyfriend in six years,

1327
01:25:10,120 --> 01:25:13,000
not since my second
breakdown.

1328
01:25:13,040 --> 01:25:15,720
I had this thing happen
on a Greyhound bus.

1329
01:25:15,720 --> 01:25:18,720
- I was--
- Do you want me to wrap that up?

1330
01:25:18,760 --> 01:25:21,400
Oh, no, thank you.

1331
01:25:21,440 --> 01:25:24,160
No, wrap it up, please.
I'll take it home.

1332
01:25:24,200 --> 01:25:26,720
Folks gonna want dessert?

1333
01:25:26,760 --> 01:25:29,720
What do you say, Sheila?
You wanna share something sweet?

1334
01:25:34,560 --> 01:25:36,880
Let's make a little stop.

1335
01:25:36,920 --> 01:25:38,640
Take the next left.

1336
01:25:45,080 --> 01:25:47,440
Turn off the lights.

1337
01:25:54,720 --> 01:25:56,760
I had
a nice time tonight.

1338
01:26:03,520 --> 01:26:05,920
The last guy
I went out with,

1339
01:26:05,960 --> 01:26:07,720
you know what he did?

1340
01:26:07,760 --> 01:26:10,520
He ditched me.

1341
01:26:10,560 --> 01:26:13,440
He got up to go
to the men's room,

1342
01:26:13,480 --> 01:26:15,440
never came back.

1343
01:26:17,000 --> 01:26:20,560
Stuck me with the check.

1344
01:26:20,600 --> 01:26:22,960
Never said goodbye.

1345
01:26:23,000 --> 01:26:25,920
Never called
to apologize.

1346
01:26:25,960 --> 01:26:29,360
He wasn't my type anyway.
He was a big-shot CPA.

1347
01:26:29,400 --> 01:26:32,760
Super normal guy.
Didn't want to be dating some psycho.

1348
01:26:40,160 --> 01:26:42,280
But you seem like
a nice person.

1349
01:27:09,960 --> 01:27:12,640
Better not tell on me.

1350
01:27:15,760 --> 01:27:17,680
You hear me?

1351
01:27:21,320 --> 01:27:23,840
You better not tell,
or I'll fucking get you.

1352
01:28:22,160 --> 01:28:24,840
Hold on, Mom.
Hold on, Dad.

1353
01:28:24,880 --> 01:28:28,360
You should park the car
on the escalator, and back...

1354
01:28:28,400 --> 01:28:30,720
- So?
- What?

1355
01:28:30,760 --> 01:28:34,560
- Up, down and then up.
- Test, dummy. How'd it go?

1356
01:28:34,600 --> 01:28:36,480
It was all right.

1357
01:28:38,560 --> 01:28:40,520
You didn't call home
last night.

1358
01:28:40,560 --> 01:28:42,120
I was worried.

1359
01:28:42,160 --> 01:28:44,160
Go on the escalator.

1360
01:28:44,200 --> 01:28:46,160
Guess I could really use
that cellphone.

1361
01:28:47,480 --> 01:28:52,120
Go on the escalator.

1362
01:29:19,680 --> 01:29:22,360
- Well, look who's back.
- Oh, hi.

1363
01:29:22,400 --> 01:29:24,640
Hi, Jean.

1364
01:29:24,680 --> 01:29:26,560
Thanks.

1365
01:29:26,600 --> 01:29:28,440
You're a lifesaver.

1366
01:29:28,480 --> 01:29:30,360
So how was
your old roommate?

1367
01:29:30,400 --> 01:29:31,720
Oh, great.

1368
01:29:31,720 --> 01:29:34,520
Yeah, thanks for doing this
on such short notice.

1369
01:29:34,560 --> 01:29:37,800
Let me give you something
for your time.

1370
01:29:37,800 --> 01:29:40,040
That's not necessary.

1371
01:29:40,040 --> 01:29:43,280
No, really.
I insist.

1372
01:29:44,560 --> 01:29:46,120
Please don't.

1373
01:29:49,200 --> 01:29:50,760
Okay.

1374
01:29:50,800 --> 01:29:53,000
Uh, any calls?

1375
01:29:53,040 --> 01:29:54,560
Nope,
it was very quiet.

1376
01:29:54,600 --> 01:29:57,920
She's asleep on your bed.
It was a very busy day.

1377
01:29:57,960 --> 01:29:59,720
That's great.

1378
01:29:59,760 --> 01:30:02,720
Um, Jean,
is everything okay?

1379
01:30:06,040 --> 01:30:08,480
Yeah, she's
a wonderful child.

1380
01:30:26,920 --> 01:30:28,840
Mommy.

1381
01:30:39,400 --> 01:30:41,320
Mommy!

1382
01:30:42,440 --> 01:30:44,000
Yeah.

1383
01:30:44,040 --> 01:30:46,320
Are you coming?

1384
01:30:49,840 --> 01:30:53,240
- I have something.
- What?

1385
01:30:53,280 --> 01:30:55,320
I have something
for you.

1386
01:30:59,400 --> 01:31:01,640
Just give me
a second here, okay?

1387
01:31:28,160 --> 01:31:30,200
Brad.

1388
01:31:34,520 --> 01:31:36,880
Brad.

1389
01:31:37,960 --> 01:31:39,840
Hmm.

1390
01:31:41,480 --> 01:31:44,440
Aaron's been telling me
about his new friend Lucy.

1391
01:31:45,400 --> 01:31:47,880
She sounds like
a sweet little girl.

1392
01:31:49,320 --> 01:31:51,280
What's her mother like?

1393
01:31:55,480 --> 01:31:58,680
Nice enough, I guess.

1394
01:31:58,720 --> 01:32:01,000
I can't even
remember her name.

1395
01:32:04,680 --> 01:32:06,720
Isn't it Sarah?

1396
01:32:08,040 --> 01:32:10,600
Sarah?

1397
01:32:10,640 --> 01:32:12,920
Yeah, Sarah,

1398
01:32:12,960 --> 01:32:14,520
from the pool.

1399
01:32:14,560 --> 01:32:17,680
Her daughter's name
is Lucy.

1400
01:32:17,720 --> 01:32:20,040
Oh, Lucy's mom.
Yeah, I forgot. That's right.

1401
01:32:20,040 --> 01:32:22,720
Her name is Sarah.

1402
01:32:22,760 --> 01:32:25,800
Hmm.

1403
01:32:25,840 --> 01:32:27,920
Yeah, it might be
really nice for Aaron

1404
01:32:27,960 --> 01:32:31,040
if they came over
for dinner.

1405
01:32:34,840 --> 01:32:36,840
Okay.

1406
01:32:58,880 --> 01:33:01,320
So you're
in advertising?

1407
01:33:01,360 --> 01:33:04,080
No, that's-- that's
a common misconception.

1408
01:33:04,120 --> 01:33:06,040
I'm not in advertising.
I'm in branding.

1409
01:33:06,080 --> 01:33:08,080
And that's very different.

1410
01:33:08,120 --> 01:33:10,160
Richard's pretty high up
in the company.

1411
01:33:10,200 --> 01:33:12,080
Yeah yeah,
I have these clients

1412
01:33:12,120 --> 01:33:13,800
and they'll come to me
when they have,

1413
01:33:13,840 --> 01:33:16,040
you know, a new product
they wanna sell.

1414
01:33:16,040 --> 01:33:19,320
Like, um, these guys,
they were in a couple weeks ago.

1415
01:33:19,360 --> 01:33:22,280
They're trying to open this chain
of Chinese restaurants, right?

1416
01:33:22,320 --> 01:33:25,640
They're talking to me
and I-- I look down at the table

1417
01:33:25,640 --> 01:33:27,880
and I realize that none
of these guys were Chinese.

1418
01:33:27,920 --> 01:33:30,760
Where were they from?

1419
01:33:30,760 --> 01:33:32,800
They're a bunch of fat cats
from Tennessee.

1420
01:33:32,840 --> 01:33:35,400
They think they can start a chain
of Chinese restaurants good enough

1421
01:33:35,440 --> 01:33:37,320
to fool the average
American boob.

1422
01:33:37,360 --> 01:33:39,320
Oh, God.

1423
01:33:39,360 --> 01:33:41,840
Mmm!

1424
01:33:41,880 --> 01:33:43,720
- This is delicious.
- Isn't it?

1425
01:33:43,760 --> 01:33:46,240
Brad's a fantastic cook.

1426
01:33:46,280 --> 01:33:49,480
So you guys see these
fliers with the guy's face

1427
01:33:49,520 --> 01:33:51,280
plastered all over
our village?

1428
01:33:51,320 --> 01:33:53,800
Lots of Sturm und Drang
in our quiet little town, huh?

1429
01:33:53,840 --> 01:33:55,440
Yeah, it's crazy.

1430
01:33:55,480 --> 01:33:57,920
What I want to know is why they let
a creep like that out of prison.

1431
01:33:57,960 --> 01:34:00,680
Some of the people
going after him are just as crazy.

1432
01:34:00,720 --> 01:34:03,560
- What do you mean?
- Well, just today I heard that

1433
01:34:03,600 --> 01:34:05,680
some nut's been spray-painting
the poor guy's house,

1434
01:34:05,720 --> 01:34:08,840
lighting fires on his porch.
God knows what else.

1435
01:34:08,840 --> 01:34:10,760
- Do they know who's doing it?
- Huh-uh.

1436
01:34:10,760 --> 01:34:12,680
They think
it's some ex-cop.

1437
01:34:12,680 --> 01:34:14,680
You know, that guy
who killed that kid at the mall.

1438
01:34:14,720 --> 01:34:16,400
Yeah, I don't think
it's him.

1439
01:34:16,440 --> 01:34:19,080
You're biased.
You're friends with the guy.

1440
01:34:19,080 --> 01:34:22,000
Brad's on that Committee
of Concerned Parents

1441
01:34:22,040 --> 01:34:23,600
with him.

1442
01:34:23,640 --> 01:34:25,400
I didn't know
you were on that.

1443
01:34:25,440 --> 01:34:27,840
I play on his
football team.

1444
01:34:27,880 --> 01:34:30,960
He asked me to distribute
some fliers.

1445
01:34:31,000 --> 01:34:32,960
He's on your team.

1446
01:34:33,000 --> 01:34:35,840
You never told me that.

1447
01:34:35,880 --> 01:34:37,480
Yeah.

1448
01:34:39,720 --> 01:34:43,200
You know what's weird?
I've never even seen this McGorvey guy.

1449
01:34:43,240 --> 01:34:45,400
- We did.
- No, we didn't.

1450
01:34:45,440 --> 01:34:47,360
Not you.
Me and Brad.

1451
01:34:47,400 --> 01:34:50,320
That day at the pool,
remember?

1452
01:34:50,360 --> 01:34:53,480
Yeah, I-I
totally forgot.

1453
01:34:55,440 --> 01:34:58,560
- Well, what happened?
- Sexual tension is an elusive thing,

1454
01:34:58,600 --> 01:35:00,840
but Kathy had
pretty good radar for it.

1455
01:35:00,840 --> 01:35:04,320
It was like someone had
turned a knob a hair to the right,

1456
01:35:04,360 --> 01:35:06,800
and the radio station
clicked in so loud and clear

1457
01:35:06,840 --> 01:35:09,480
it almost
knocked her over.

1458
01:35:09,520 --> 01:35:11,880
Once she became aware
of the connection between them,

1459
01:35:11,880 --> 01:35:14,880
it seemed impossible that
she'd missed it before.

1460
01:35:14,920 --> 01:35:17,440
On a hunch,
Kathy dropped her fork

1461
01:35:17,480 --> 01:35:19,600
in the hopes that
while retrieving it

1462
01:35:19,640 --> 01:35:22,880
she would catch Sarah
and Brad playing footsie,

1463
01:35:22,920 --> 01:35:25,160
- but she was mistaken.
- Asked him to leave, and he left.

1464
01:35:25,200 --> 01:35:28,360
But you know what?
He didn't look scary, right? I mean--

1465
01:35:28,360 --> 01:35:31,080
No, it-- it wasn't that big
of a deal, you know.

1466
01:35:31,120 --> 01:35:33,720
- No, he's like a regular guy, you know?
- Yeah, I mean--

1467
01:35:33,760 --> 01:35:35,680
Kathy, are you okay?

1468
01:35:35,720 --> 01:35:37,560
Uh, yeah.

1469
01:35:37,600 --> 01:35:40,600
Just a sec.

1470
01:35:40,640 --> 01:35:42,240
Brad had convinced
himself

1471
01:35:42,280 --> 01:35:44,640
the dinner party
had gone well.

1472
01:35:44,680 --> 01:35:48,040
That he and Sarah had managed
to put Kathy's suspicions to rest,

1473
01:35:48,080 --> 01:35:50,200
at least temporarily.

1474
01:35:50,240 --> 01:35:52,360
She certainly hadn't
accused him of anything,

1475
01:35:52,360 --> 01:35:54,280
or behaved in a way
that made him think

1476
01:35:54,280 --> 01:35:56,240
she'd noticed
anything untoward.

1477
01:36:01,280 --> 01:36:04,640
I'm really glad
they came over.

1478
01:36:10,160 --> 01:36:12,960
- Good night.
- Good night.

1479
01:36:13,000 --> 01:36:14,640
Two days later, however,

1480
01:36:14,680 --> 01:36:16,880
his mother-in-law showed up
for a surprise visit...

1481
01:36:16,920 --> 01:36:19,720
- Thank you, John.
- ...of ominously indeterminate length.

1482
01:36:19,760 --> 01:36:22,440
And from that moment on,
she accompanied Brad

1483
01:36:22,480 --> 01:36:24,200
and Aaron everywhere:

1484
01:36:24,200 --> 01:36:26,000
To the playground;

1485
01:36:26,040 --> 01:36:28,840
- We need some butter.
- To the supermarket;

1486
01:36:28,880 --> 01:36:31,280
And to the town pool.

1487
01:36:31,320 --> 01:36:33,880
Hi, Aaron.

1488
01:36:33,920 --> 01:36:37,080
Hi, Lucy.

1489
01:36:38,440 --> 01:36:41,120
The worst of it
was the pool.

1490
01:36:41,160 --> 01:36:43,720
Nevertheless,
after threatening all week

1491
01:36:43,760 --> 01:36:46,920
to go the football game,
Brad's mother-in-law decided against it

1492
01:36:46,920 --> 01:36:48,840
at the last minute.

1493
01:36:48,840 --> 01:36:50,840
- So are you coming?
- Well, I'd like to,

1494
01:36:50,880 --> 01:36:52,760
but I'm a little tired.

1495
01:36:52,800 --> 01:36:55,040
You sure?
You're welcome to.

1496
01:36:55,080 --> 01:36:56,960
No, go ahead.
You and Aaron

1497
01:36:57,000 --> 01:36:59,160
sure kept me hopping
this afternoon.

1498
01:36:59,200 --> 01:37:01,200
Looks like
you're on your own.

1499
01:37:01,240 --> 01:37:04,080
Oh, that's my ride.

1500
01:37:04,120 --> 01:37:06,760
- What time are you gonna be home?
- I don't know.

1501
01:37:06,760 --> 01:37:08,440
Pretty late, though.

1502
01:37:08,480 --> 01:37:11,320
Be careful and stay
out of trouble.

1503
01:37:23,920 --> 01:37:26,320
Winds whisper
of high hopes,

1504
01:37:26,360 --> 01:37:28,400
victory is in the skies.

1505
01:37:28,440 --> 01:37:31,680
One joins with many
on the summer's green field.

1506
01:37:31,720 --> 01:37:34,760
At 0 and 5, the Guardians
were the basement dwellers

1507
01:37:34,760 --> 01:37:37,480
of the Tri-County
Touch Football Night League.

1508
01:37:37,520 --> 01:37:41,240
The Controllers, a team of young
hotshots from the financial district

1509
01:37:41,280 --> 01:37:44,040
were 4 and 1
with an explosive offense

1510
01:37:44,080 --> 01:37:47,240
that regularly racked up
40 to 50 points a game.

1511
01:37:47,240 --> 01:37:49,280
But from
the opening kickoff,

1512
01:37:49,320 --> 01:37:52,680
this ragtag group
of law enforcement officers

1513
01:37:52,720 --> 01:37:54,840
decided to crunch
their own numbers.

1514
01:37:58,400 --> 01:38:00,600
But the Controllers
scored first

1515
01:38:00,640 --> 01:38:02,560
after recovering
a Bart Williams fumble

1516
01:38:02,600 --> 01:38:06,360
deep in Guardians' territory near
the end of the second quarter.

1517
01:38:10,920 --> 01:38:13,880
The Guardians evened things up early
